# Complete Fabric Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart TD

    A[Business Requirement] --> B[Data Sources]

    B --> C[Data Factory / Pipeline]
    B --> D[Dataflow Gen2]

    C --> E[Bronze Layer]
    D --> E

    E --> F[Notebook / PySpark]
    F --> G[Silver Layer]

    G --> H[Business Transformations]
    H --> I[Gold Layer]

    I --> J[Lakehouse]
    I --> K[Fabric Warehouse]

    J --> L[Semantic Model]
    K --> L

    L --> M[Power BI Report]
    M --> N[Business Users]

    J -. Data Reuse .-> O[OneLake Shortcuts]

    N --> P[Monitoring]
    N --> Q[Security & Governance]
```

---

# Quick Revision Path

If I need a fast revision before an interview:

**Fabric Fundamentals**  
→ **Lakehouse & Delta**  
→ **Pipelines**  
→ **PySpark / Dataflow Gen2**  
→ **Bronze–Silver–Gold**  
→ **Lakehouse vs Warehouse**  
→ **Star Schema**  
→ **Semantic Model & Power BI**  
→ **Monitoring**  
→ **Security & Governance**

---

# Core Mental Model

> **OneLake stores the data.**  
> **Capacity provides the compute.**  
> **Workspaces organize the solution.**  
> **Pipelines orchestrate the workflow.**  
> **Dataflow Gen2 performs low-code transformations.**  
> **Notebooks perform code-based transformations.**  
> **Lakehouse supports flexible data engineering.**  
> **Warehouse supports structured SQL analytics.**  
> **Semantic Models provide business meaning.**  
> **Power BI communicates insights.**  
> **Monitoring keeps the solution observable.**  
> **Security & Governance keep the solution controlled.**

---

## Repository Purpose

This repository is my personal reference for understanding Microsoft Fabric architecture and analytics workflows.

Detailed implementation of individual projects is maintained separately under the **Projects** section of this repository.
